Z||000003||MrPBody||12-05-2000||09:17 AM||||A "universal" 3/8"-drive piston return tool is about $10 at most parts stores.  I haven't actually used mine yet, so I can't guarantee it fits.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||horseman||12-06-2000||01:29 AM||||NOT my idea but, I read that you can use calipers from the fronts of 84-87 on the rear of those same years. The guy said that they bolted right up and really increased his stopping power. If this is true then this would be the ultimate in a cheap brake upgrade! Has anybody heard of this before?<BR>I will try and find the website.<BR>Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000005||horseman||12-06-2000||01:34 AM||||Here is the url for the brakes <A HREF="http://www.fierotech.net/cheaprearbrakes.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierotech.net/cheaprearbrakes.htm</A> <BR>Hope this helps. Let me know as I might even do this to my 84.<BR>Charlie||||reg||4
Z||000006||Carrolles||12-06-2000||06:15 AM||||Horse,<P>Keep in mind that you'll lose the e-brake if you use front calipers on the back.<P>Take care,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000007||Zorba||12-06-2000||12:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>definatly autozone.. $31.99!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I tried theirs and they would not fit. Without trimming the caliper housing (which I did not want to do in case I needed to return them) they would not fit. The mounting holes were milimeters off and for 3 hours I could not get them to fit!!!<BR>So I went out and bought 88 grand am calipers. 10 minutes and I had everthing bolted on! Could not figure this one out. I have replaced 3-4 calipers on the fiero before and NEVER had the problem I did with autozone's calipers! It was like the caliper body needed to go up about 1/16" inch in order for the mounting holes to match! <BR>After much sweat and cursing I said "F this!" and went with the grand ams but now I am being told that this is not such a good idea. For the moment I don't care abou the ebrake. My car is already inspected for another year.<BR>Checker has the cardena brand at $40 /each for the 86 4 cylinder SE. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000008||kslish||12-06-2000||05:40 PM||||I've got Autozone rear calipers on my GT that I put on a few months ago.  They fit great.  They may have accidentally given you a pair of calipers for another type of car that were either in the wrong boxes or the box was mislabled (it happens).<P>Take them back and have them compare another new set with your old calipers to make sure they are the same.<P>Ken S.<BR>'86 SC and '87 GT<BR> <A HREF="http://www.westpennfiero.org" TARGET=_blank>www.westpennfiero.org</A> ||||reg||3
Z||000009||Firefly||06-19-2001||03:31 PM||||what do you need to do to push the rear calipers piston back in?<P>what tool, turn what way and do I need to take the e-brake cable off?||||reg||5
Z||000010||Steven||06-19-2001||04:18 PM||||I have a '85 GT with front calipers mounted on the rear. Had no problem getting them to bolt up, just had to make sure that the rear rotors where turned down to the same size as brand new front rotors (remember front rotors are thinner than rear rotors). Rear rotors were turned down to .430 inch. Also remember the left front caliper gets mounted to the right rear of the car, and the right front calipes goes on the left rear of the car. From that point on if you bought brake pads with life time warrenty, any time you took a wheel off, you can put new pads on since frt and rr pads would now be the same (replace in sets, don't just change the left or right).<P>As for the park brake, if you have an automatic trans, the brake is not an issue (unless you live somewhere that has state inspections). If you have a manual trans remember to park with the car in reverse. <P>This is just what I did, and I have not had any problems yet. You do whatever it is that you want to do. I am not telling you to do this. Just be careful, and enjoy your ride.<P>------------------<BR>85 Fiero GT<BR>3.2 liter w/4sp manual trans<BR>Silver.<p>[This message has been edited by Steven (edited 06-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Firefly||06-20-2001||01:18 PM||||autozone.com<BR>rear caliper  $100 / $80 core<BR>front caliper $ 28 / $59 core<P>rockauto.com<BR>rear caliper  $61.19 / $50 core<BR>front caliper $ 27.25 / $15 core<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||reholmes||08-25-2001||12:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Firefly:<BR><B>autozone.com<BR>rear caliper  $100 / $80 core<BR>front caliper $ 28 / $59 core<P>rockauto.com<BR>rear caliper  $61.19 / $50 core<BR>front caliper $ 27.25 / $15 core</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The prices have definitely gone up at the "Zone", I paid $90 plus core about a month ago. My brakes, '87 GT auto, still aren't doing well. Bled completely, adjusted the e-brake, good pads all around, still can't lock 'em up. Any suggestions?<BR>||||reg||1
